# Line endings, so the declared formatter is runnable on the machine it is run on.
#
# `.prettierrc` sets `endOfLine: "lf"`. Without this file, git honours whatever
# `core.autocrlf` the developer happens to have: on Windows that is `true`, so the
# checkout writes CRLF while the repository stores LF. `prettier --check` then
# reports every single file as unformatted -- 337 of 337 -- and every one of those
# reports is about a carriage return, not about the code. A gate that fails on
# everything is a gate nobody can read, so nobody ran it, and the two real
# findings it had were buried under the other 335.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises on commit and checks out LF everywhere,
# regardless of `core.autocrlf`. The stored content does not change: the index is
# already LF, which is why this lands as one file and not as a rewrite of the tree.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, and must never be touched: a substituted byte corrupts the file rather
# than reformatting it.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.eot binary
*.pdf binary
*.docx binary
*.xlsx binary
